                <pb facs="0750" n="648" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg648"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.003/454: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Reinsch</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-02-02T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 2,
                                1918, 4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Your January 18, 7 p.m. and January 23, 6 p.m. Question of the immediate
                    institution of surtax on customs duties or [<hi rendition="#italic">of?</hi>]
                    flat 5 per cent rate now being considered by the Department.</p>
                <pb facs="0751" n="649" xml:id="pg649"/>
                <p>Other questions raised in above mentioned telegrams are answered by Department’s
                    December 13, 6 p.m., which please communicate to Arnold if this has not already
                    been done.</p>
                <p>Department disposed to favor valuations based on period 1913 to 1917, inclusive,
                    but believes it would be best to leave such questions to the judgment of the
                    Conference. Policy of the Department is to leave, as far as possible, all
                    questions concerning the revision of the tariff to the Conference. Interference
                    on the part of interested governments, except where necessity seems to make
                    recommendations imperative, will greatly handicap the Delegates in the
                    expeditious and careful handling of their work.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
